comments[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-30T10:37:49.913Z","harness:claude-code","Reproduced, fixed, and gated on 2026-08-30.\n\nReproduction, before the fix, against the released stdio entrypoint plugins/pm-claude/scripts/pm-mcp-server.mjs:\n- initialize protocolVersion 2025-11-25 (what Claude Code 2.1.251 sends, captured on the wire through a logging shim) returned -32022 with data.supported ['2025-06-18']; the host reported 'Failed to connect'.\n- initialize with 2024-11-05, 2025-03-26 and 2026-07-28 were refused identically, so the server refused its own canonical revision on the entrypoint every legacy-era host opens.\n- server/discover carrying _meta protocolVersion 2026-07-28 returned a complete DiscoverResult with supportedVersions ['2026-07-28'] and three negotiated extensions, proving the capability existed and was unreachable through the handshake rather than absent.\n\nRoot cause: PM_MCP_LEGACY_PROTOCOL_VERSIONS was the single literal ['2025-06-18']. The canonical revision's Versioning and Compatibility page defines legacy as the initialize-handshake versions, 2025-11-25 and earlier, and its compatibility matrix promises that a legacy client against a dual-era server works and is served according to the negotiated legacy revision. The accept list was a hand-maintained subset of that boundary and nothing compared the two. The adapter also answered PM_MCP_LEGACY_PROTOCOL_VERSIONS[0] rather than the requested revision, so widening the set alone would still have stranded a client on a version it did not negotiate.\n\nFix: the accept set now carries the full initialize-era boundary newest-first; resolveLegacyProtocolVersion echoes the negotiated revision, offers the newest revision when protocolVersion is omitted, and refuses anything else with -32022 naming every supported revision, because a legacy client has no fall-forward mechanism and that error text is its only diagnostic.\n\nVerification after the fix:\n- initialize 2025-11-25 negotiates 2025-11-25; initialize 2025-06-18 negotiates 2025-06-18; initialize 2024-11-05 is refused with supported ['2025-11-25','2025-06-18'].\n- claude mcp list now reports pm-mcp Connected, end to end from the same host that failed.\n- tests/integration/mcp-handshake.spec.ts 40 passed, tests/integration/mcp-stateless-protocol.spec.ts 12 passed, tests/unit/sdk/mcp/protocol.spec.ts 6 passed, plugin smoke unit specs 30 passed.\n- Both real-process smokes print 'protocol handshake matrix: ok (negotiated 2025-11-25, 2025-06-18; unsupported refused)'.\n\nGate: assertProtocolHandshakeMatrix reads the revision list from the built SDK by dynamic import rather than restating it, so the matrix iterates the artifact the transport actually accepts instead of a second hand list. That is the property the pre-fix code lacked. Negative control run explicitly: declaring an extra revision 2025-03-26 that the transport does not accept makes the matrix fail with 'Unsupported legacy MCP protocol version' rather than passing, so the gate can fail for the reason it exists.\n\nThe sdk:surface gate refused the constant change until a breaking-change reason was recorded, which is the surface governance working as designed; the acknowledgement is in sdk/public-surface.json."
  "2026-08-30T12:35:18.554Z","harness:claude-code","Hosted CI follow-up for a326ad61f, resolved in the next commit.\n\nTwo gates failed hosted that the local pass had not exercised. Gates (static) failed on ESLint, which pnpm quality:static does not run locally: assertProtocolHandshakeMatrix scored complexity 20 against a maximum of 16, and a let initialiser was flagged as a useless assignment. Gates (coverage) failed because the new matrix code sat at 58 percent line coverage inside a file whose exports had only spawn-plumbing tests.\n\nBoth are now fixed rather than waived. The matrix is decomposed into negotiateLegacyRevision, assertUndeclaredRevisionRefused and assertModernRevisionDiscoverable, which drops it under the complexity ceiling and makes each property independently testable. Nine tests were added over the mocked child: the full declared-set walk, the SDK-derived fallback when no list is supplied, the empty-list refusal, an answered revision the client did not request, a handshake without server identity, an accepted undeclared revision, a non-protocol rejection that must rethrow, discovery omitting the canonical revision, and discovery returning no result. One genuinely dead branch was removed rather than covered: the harness rejects only with Error instances, so the non-Error arm of the refusal message was unreachable.\n\nLocal verification after the fix: coverage 100/100/100/100 with zero uncovered counts, eslint . --max-warnings=0 clean, pnpm quality:static exit 0, both real-process smokes print the matrix line, and the declared-but-unaccepted-revision negative control still fails for the right reason.\n\nWorth recording as a method note: the local preflight named by the contributing docs does not include the lint step that hosted CI runs, so a change can pass every local gate and fail Gates (static) on lint alone. That is a smaller instance of the local-versus-hosted divergence tracked on pm-4o7tlx."
